WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.700
ROGER MARTINEZ: In
today's IT world,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.010
most developers wear
many different hats,

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.450
and you're probably
no exception.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.180
But managing
applications designed

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.450
for different platforms
can be quite a chore.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.870
The problem is it's hard to
handle all the architecture

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.330
and upkeep required
to do such a task.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.628
For example, dealing with
your own provisioning.

00:00:17.628 --> 00:00:19.170
Not to mention all
the work to ensure

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.060
that everything is secure.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.550
What if you could wrap your
workloads in a common package,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.470
like docker containers,
that you can run

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.110
on prem and in any major cloud?

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.800
Thankfully, this is exactly
what Google Cloud Run is for.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.400
Cloud Run is a fully managed
serverless compute platform.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.920
It automatically scales and
load balances your stateless

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.240
containers on a pay
per request basis

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.120
so you can do what
matters most--

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.310
build great applications.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.620
It's driven by portable
Docker-based images that

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.890
can run almost anywhere, letting
you leverage the best of what

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.390
you need in the cloud.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.272
Containerization lets
you have full control

00:00:53.272 --> 00:00:55.230
over your runtime
environment without giving up

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.165
the benefits of serverless.

00:00:57.165 --> 00:00:59.040
Whether you're a pro at
containers or looking

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.780
to build your first
containerized app,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.820
Cloud Run lets you get your
code into production in seconds.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.090
Simply run a gcloud command
to deploy your stateless

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.000
container, and Cloud
Run will do the rest.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.150
Listen to requests and events,
pass them to your code,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.450
and scale them up and
down to meet user demand.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.470
The best part?

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.500
You can do all this in
your favorite language,

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.822
and you only pay for the
requests that you process.

00:01:20.822 --> 00:01:22.530
Cloud Run is a great
solution for running

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.420
your containerized
HTTP applications

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.850
if you don't have specialized
DevOps requirements.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.363
But why stop there?

00:01:28.363 --> 00:01:30.780
Cloud Run can be used to help
multiple kinds of workloads,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.210
not just HTTP-based ones.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.030
Let's take a closer look at
what you can do with Cloud Run.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.010
The first major use
case for Cloud Run

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.050
is event orchestration.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.640
Cloud Run has the
ability to listen

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.710
to events from other
Cloud components,

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.200
handle webhooks, and
manage distributing

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.780
events and workloads
to other components.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.760
This built-in ability
makes it straightforward

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.790
for your application to
handle complex event needs.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.650
Another good set of
workloads for Cloud Run

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.468
involves data processing.

00:01:57.468 --> 00:01:59.010
When you need to
adjust and transform

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.120
large amounts of data,
Cloud Run provides

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.730
the infrastructure
your workloads need

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:06.030
to handle auto-scaling,
authorization, and event

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.789
triggers via a Pub/Sub
model of communication.

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:10.985
Cloud Run leverages the
power of Google Cloud

00:02:10.985 --> 00:02:12.360
to provide these
features for you

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.070
so you can build
complex scalable data

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:17.530
pipelines while saving time
spent on backend confusion.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.230
Of course, this
type of flexibility

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.510
also makes building
microservices and developing

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.490
APIs a lot easier.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.010
Simply configure your stateless
container to listen for

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.195
and handle ATP requests,
webhooks, or CloudEvents,

00:02:29.195 --> 00:02:31.350
and you've got the ability
to deploy a federation

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.390
of microservices in no time.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.700
Advanced features, such
as traffic splitting,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.440
audit logging, and
custom domains,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.000
are built in for
when you need them.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.060
And with the power of Google
Cloud to client APIs and Cloud

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.760
workflows, Cloud Run
becomes a powerful tool

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.980
to help you automate
complex processes that

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.890
require coordination of
distributed services and APIs,

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:53.650
like G Suite or Google
Cloud's ML and AI offerings.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:55.650
If you're looking to build
a mobile or web-based

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.420
application, then you
know how important

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.910
a fast development cycle and
native local host developer

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:01.080
experience are.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.630
Cloud Run makes creating
client-side frontend services

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.780
easy, and seamlessly interfaces
with storage, databases,

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.580
and other services
on the back end.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.190
All at the same power,
scalability, and demand,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.470
regardless of your
traffic profile.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.770
So whether you're a seasoned
IT professional or just

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.440
discovering the flexibility
of container-based workloads,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.450
Cloud Run is right
there to help you build

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.342
great portable applications.

00:03:23.342 --> 00:03:25.050
For more information,
check out the links

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.177
in the description below.

00:03:26.177 --> 00:03:28.260
And don't forget to subscribe
to our Cloud YouTube

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.960
channel for more great content
on building your application.

